1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,725
An AI agent has to pay for APIs and data. Give it a wallet, and one leaked key drains the wallet. Here, the agent never holds the money.

2
00:00:09,725 --> 00:00:20,069
A lender vault pays instead. It pays only vendors the sponsor approved, within limits the sponsor sets. Every check runs before a token moves.

3
00:00:20,069 --> 00:00:25,748
The sponsor locked four hundred in collateral and opened one credit line for one agent.

4
00:00:25,748 --> 00:00:35,117
This page simulates the vault's rules in the browser. Debt is zero. The agent can borrow up to one hundred, and forty in any one channel.

5
00:00:35,117 --> 00:00:38,938
First, the agent asks the vault to pay its own wallet.

6
00:00:38,938 --> 00:00:44,258
The vault refuses. The sponsor never approved that address, so nothing moves.

7
00:00:44,258 --> 00:00:49,030
Next, an approved vendor, but forty one dollars. The cap is forty.

8
00:00:49,030 --> 00:00:54,222
Refused again. The limits live in the contract, not in the agent's promise.

9
00:00:54,222 --> 00:00:56,531
Thirty is inside the cap.

10
00:00:56,531 --> 00:01:04,883
The vault escrows thirty in an x402 payment channel for the vendor. The agent never touches it. It only signs vouchers.

11
00:01:04,883 --> 00:01:12,880
Now a customer pays the agent one hundred, through the vault. Thirty repays the loan first. Seventy reaches the agent.

12
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:24,623
Underneath, this is the real x402 batch settlement contract on Monad, run on a local fork. Eight of eight attempts outside the policy were refused with the expected error.

13
00:01:24,623 --> 00:01:37,473
Every repayment builds a record. After three repaid rounds, the same collateral supported two hundred forty five in credit, up from one hundred. The contracts and tests are in the repository.

